## 🔍 What is **Spring Auditing**?

**Spring Data JPA Auditing** helps you **automatically track**:

* **Who** created or updated an entity
* **When** it was created or updated

This is very useful in real-world applications for **audit trails**—to monitor data changes over time.

---

## ✅ Example Use Case

You want to store in each DB record:

* `createdBy`, `createdDate`
* `lastModifiedBy`, `lastModifiedDate`

Instead of manually setting them every time, Spring will automatically populate them.

---

## ✅ Steps to Enable Spring Auditing

---

### 🟩 **STEP 1: Enable Auditing in Main Class**

Add the annotation:

```java
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableJpaAuditing(auditorAwareRef = "auditorProvider") // "auditorProvider" is the bean name
public class YourApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(YourApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

---

### 🟩 **STEP 2: Add Audit Fields to Entity**

```java
@Entity
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
public class MyEntity {

    @CreatedBy
    @Column(updatable = false)
    private Integer createdBy;

    @CreatedDate
    @Column(updatable = false)
    private LocalDateTime createdDate;

    @LastModifiedBy
    @Column(insertable = false)
    private Integer modifiedBy;

    @LastModifiedDate
    @Column(insertable = false)
    private LocalDateTime modifiedDate;

    // other fields, getters/setters
}
```

> ✅ `@CreatedDate` and `@CreatedBy` → set only when entity is created
> ✅ `@LastModifiedDate` and `@LastModifiedBy` → set whenever the entity is updated

---

### 🟩 **STEP 3: Provide Auditor Info**

Create a class that tells Spring **who is the current user** (i.e., `createdBy`, `modifiedBy`).

```java
@Configuration
public class AuditorConfig {

    @Bean
    public AuditorAware<Integer> auditorProvider() {
        return new AuditorAwareImpl();
    }
}
```

Now, create the implementation:

```java
public class AuditorAwareImpl implements AuditorAware<Integer> {

    @Override
    public Optional<Integer> getCurrentAuditor() {
        // Example: return static user ID = 1
        return Optional.of(1);

        // In real projects: fetch from SecurityContext
        // return Optional.of(SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication().getName());
    }
}
```

---

## ✅ Final Summary

| Annotation              | Meaning                           |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `@EnableJpaAuditing`    | Enables auditing support          |
| `@EntityListeners(...)` | Hooks entity lifecycle            |
| `@CreatedDate`          | Auto-filled creation timestamp    |
| `@CreatedBy`            | Auto-filled creator ID            |
| `@LastModifiedDate`     | Auto-filled last update timestamp |
| `@LastModifiedBy`       | Auto-filled modifier ID           |

